James W. McKelvey <mckelvey@maskull.com> wrote:

> I'm considering buying an Alpha workstation from Microway. It would be a 
> low-end system (by Alpha standards) 21164 at 667 MHz. 64M, 2M cache for 
> roughly $3200 sans monitor. It would run RedHat Linux.

I bought a high-end dp264 from them.  I'm quite happy.  The biggest
problem is that at the high end, Linux is not quite ready for
large memory (4GB), even with the BigMEM patches.  Otherwise,
it's very stable and pretty well everything that's great about Linux
on a PC is also great on an Alpha.

> Is that a good idea? bad idea?

For what you're spec'ing you get a whole lot more FP performance
than a comparable PIII or AMD, also far greater memory bandwidth,
as well as (of course) 64 bit support.  It's more money, but by
the time you add in SCSI and some other high-end features that are
standard in an Alpha, it's not that much more than a souped up Intel
system.

> The primary justification is that I want a fast machine for compiling 
> template-heavy C++. It also has to serve as a general-purpose home machine 
> for mail, news, internet, etc.

On an Alpha, you can also use Compaq's compiler (not just g++).  I've
barely any experience with this yet, but it's free for non-commercial
use.  g++ does OK, but as others have pointed out, can be slow for
template-heavy stuff (I've noticed this a lot).  Raw CPU speed with
enough memory will address this problem; I don't think an Alpha v. 
Pentium/AMD CPU makes much difference.

My 700 Mhz DP264 Alpha system is something like twice as
fast as my 700 Mhz AMD system at compiling, both running RedHat 6.2.
This is for code that makes extensive use of g++ STL template classes.

> I'd appreciate hearing about anyone's experiences.

Microway is a good company with solid tech support.  Their staff
is generally quite competent, and they really do specialize at the
higher-end market (e.g., not just PCs).  I recommend them.

When I decided to buy an Alpha, I looked for other companies, and
basically it's Microway (a VAR for Compaq) or Compaq.  None of
the other VARs have even updated their Web pages recently, and
when we put the system out for bid only Microway responded.  If
there are other vendors worth looking at, I'd appreciate hearing about
them.

The main problem with Alphas is that they're not keeping up with the
technology curve.  If they don't have some serious advances in CPU
speed and smaller die size over the next year, they'll not be a contender
any more.

Bur for now, an Alpha 700Mhz is about the same performance as a PIII
or AMD 1GHz chip for scientific stuff (e.g., matrix multiplications
etc.).  This is NOT a gaming chip, it's really only worth the $$$ for
scientific or similar types of things.

