I agree, they kind of dropped the ball floating point wise, they could
really be kicking ass right now.  They do have their motion vector unit 
in the EV6/264, but it's not as nice as the one in the G4.  Aside from
the better MHz, branch prediction and fatter pipes, the FPU is about
the same as it was in the EV4.  Same ADD/everything else + MUL unit that
it has been all along.  I've never seen anything finalized for the EV7
but I hope that if they have two or more multi-functional FPUs, they
also break full backwards compatibility by giving us more registers.
That 9 stage MUL pipe eats a tremendous number of registers to keep
intermediate results around, if we had two MUL's going at once, we'd
have a hard time keeping the results handy.  Which is the basic problem
with the x86 FPUs, not enough registers.

But anyway, the "business intelligence" and OLAP markets are getting
bigger, I'm sure that Compaq/API would like to sell more big clusters.
Kicking ass at floating point is also good with the extreme gamers.

I think Alpha will be OK going into the 21st century.

