You do this with the xsetroot command.

Here's a sample cursor file:

======>pointer.xbm<======

#define pointer_width 16
#define pointer_height 16
#define pointer_x_hot 1
#define pointer_y_hot 1
static unsigned char pointer_bits[] = {
   0x00, 0x00, 0x02, 0x00, 0x0c, 0x00, 0x3c, 0x00, 0xf8, 0x00, 0xf8, 0x03,
   0xf0, 0x0f, 0xf0, 0x00, 0x60, 0x01, 0x60, 0x02, 0x40, 0x04, 0x40, 0x08,
   0x00, 0x10, 0x00, 0x20, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00};

and a sample mask file:

======>pointermask.xbm<======

#define pointermask_width 16
#define pointermask_height 16
static unsigned char pointermask_bits[] = {
   0x03, 0x00, 0x0f, 0x00, 0x3e, 0x00, 0xfe, 0x00, 0xfc, 0x03, 0xfc, 0x0f,
   0xf8, 0x1f, 0xf8, 0x0f, 0xf0, 0x03, 0xf0, 0x07, 0xe0, 0x0e, 0xe0, 0x1c,
   0x40, 0x38, 0x00, 0x70, 0x00, 0x20, 0x00, 0x00};


These can be edited in the 'bitmap' program.  Then you just run:

xsetroot -cursor pointer.xbm pointermask.xbm

Regards,

	Tony Hammitt


