Here are a few Q&As that you might find useful:

-Q- What is an SRM console?
-A- Forget about what the acronym stands for, it doesn't seem to have much 
meaning.  Most times, instead of saying the correct term "SRM console," we 
simply say "SRM," and imply that we're talking about the console.  You can 
recognize the SRM console by its distinctive >>> prompt.  The important thing 
to know about it is, it's a miniature OS, which is very UNIX-like, with a hint 
of VMS.  It resides in the firmware, and serves the same purpose as the BIOS 
setup commonly used in x86 based PCs; but the SRM console is far more powerful.  
The most common uses for SRM are for basic functionality testing, and for 
launching boot code from the device of your choice.

-Q- How can I have a command prompt when the CPU is halted?
-A- The SRM prompt is controlled in the firmware, not the CPU.  When you're 
executing commands on the SRM prompt, the firmware circuits perform the 
operations, leaving the CPU(s) inactive.  That's why the SRM seems so slow!!  
;-)   (if you noticed.)

-Q- What's the difference between SRM, ARC, and AlphaBIOS?  How do I know which 
one is right for me?
-A- It's hard to say the fundamental difference without using details.  
Especially since I don't know any details.  AlphaBIOS and ARC are generally 
used for loading Windows NT, while SRM is generally used for Unixes, Linuxes, 
and VMS.  In some cases, you might need to use both SRM and AlphaBIOS, or 
perhaps ARC and AlphaBIOS.  I have to admit, I don't have much experience with 
this one, but I can say this:
The vast majority of people using Alphas will be happy using SRM and forgetting 
about AlphaBIOS or ARC.

-Q- Where can I obtain firmware updates for the SRM, ARC, or AlphaBIOS?
-A- As of Dec, 2000, http://ftp.digital.com/pub/DEC/Alpha/firmware/
Alternatively, this is how I found the above link:
Go to www.compaq.com
Go to Support, Software and Drivers, Alpha Firmware, and select your machine.

-Q- How do I change from ARC to SRM, or vice-versa?
-A- If you're in SRM going to ARC, type in
	>>> set os_type nt
	Power cycle your system.
If you're in ARC going to SRM, do the following:
	From the boot menu, select "Supplementary menu"
	Select "Set up the system..."
	Near the bottom, select "Switch to OpenVMS or Digital UNIX console"
	It says your boot selection is currently NT.  
	Select either UNIX or VMS, it doesn't matter too much
	It says your boot selection is currently... whatever you chose
	Select "Setup menu..."
	You will be prompted to power cycle your system.

-Q- How do I change to AlphaBIOS?
-A- This answer will be very vague, as I have not the ability to do it myself.
From SRM, type alphabios and press enter.  Only works on some machines.
From ARC, I don't know.
Why would you use AlphaBIOS?  I don't know either.


