That's how it looks and feels, but actually SRM is run in Alpha processor.
Like you wrote SRM is miniature (unix-like) operating system. Would be
quite a challence to execute that in simpler circuits.
Halt instruction is not really a halt of processor, but a call to
PALcode instruction halt (microcoded sequence of instructions), which then
may transfer control to SRM program.
So the halted processor in not real but merely virtual. SRM keeps the
program address etc. safe so that "virtual processor" can be restarted
(>>> continue)
I guess slowliness is mainly because of poor terminal emulation in SRM. 

