> Here are a few Q&As that you might find useful:
>
> -Q- What is an SRM console?
> -A- Forget about what the acronym stands for, it doesn't seem to have much
> meaning.  Most times, instead of saying the correct term "SRM console," we
> simply say "SRM," and imply that we're talking about the console.  You can
> recognize the SRM console by its distinctive >>> prompt.  The important
thing
> to know about it is, it's a miniature OS, which is very UNIX-like, with a
hint
> of VMS.  It resides in the firmware, and serves the same purpose as the
BIOS
> setup commonly used in x86 based PCs; but the SRM console is far more
powerful.
> The most common uses for SRM are for basic functionality testing, and for
> launching boot code from the device of your choice.
>
> -Q- How can I have a command prompt when the CPU is halted?
> -A- The SRM prompt is controlled in the firmware, not the CPU.  When
you're
> executing commands on the SRM prompt, the firmware circuits perform the
> operations, leaving the CPU(s) inactive.  That's why the SRM seems so
slow!!
> ;-)   (if you noticed.)
>
> -Q- What's the difference between SRM, ARC, and AlphaBIOS?  How do I know
which
> one is right for me?
> -A- It's hard to say the fundamental difference without using details.
> Especially since I don't know any details.  AlphaBIOS and ARC are
generally
> used for loading Windows NT, while SRM is generally used for Unixes,
Linuxes,
> and VMS.  In some cases, you might need to use both SRM and AlphaBIOS, or
> perhaps ARC and AlphaBIOS.  I have to admit, I don't have much experience
with
> this one, but I can say this:
> The vast majority of people using Alphas will be happy using SRM and
forgetting
> about AlphaBIOS or ARC.
>
> -Q- Where can I obtain firmware updates for the SRM, ARC, or AlphaBIOS?
