ralf.naegele@she.net wrote:
> Since I think the memory of my Alpha 21164 seems to be broken
> (it crashes from time to time) I want to buy new memory. As
> I contacted my hardware dealer he said for which Alpha the
> memory should be: A 21164 SX or LX? 
> And that's the problem: How can I determine what version my
> Alpha is? The manual for the motherboard says nothing about it.

> What I surely know is the following:

> * AlphaPC164
> * CPU Alpha 21164 500 MHz
> * All SIMMs must be 36-bit and have 70ns or faster access time
> * All SIMMs must be of equal size

> I think it's a Alpha 21164 SX but I'm not sure about it.

No, it's not. As many have replied already, it's a PC164 board.
These take ( as opposed to previous replies ) True parity SIMMS of
"PS/2" style - as they are oftern referred to.
IIRC, it does not matter if these are Fast-Pagemode or EDO type,
since the latter offers fast-page signalling.

The point is, that nobody on this planet actually makes those modules
anymore. The only PS/2 style SIMMS today offer either no parity
at all, or a so called "fake-parity", which does not work in these
boards.
You have to use them in sets of four of equal size.

Your best chance of getting them, would be places like ebay or some
used-equipment dealer. maybe some large memory-vender still has some
in stock. Your User Manual, that should have come with the board,
will contain a list of memory-modules for reference.
Since you are in Germany ( as i am ), you might want to check
www.request.nl in the netherlands. They usually carry memory of this
type, AFAIK ( Note: I'm not affiliated to them at all )

If you don't have that manual, go to:

www.support.compaq.com/alpha-tools/documentation/current/chip-docs.html

and look for the Alpha PC164 table on that page. There, you can download
all docs relating to your motherboard.

If you happen to have SRM instead of ARC as a firmware, you can test
the system memory at firmware-level, which should give you an idea, if
the memory is bad or not. Feel free to contact me ( or this group ) for
assistance, if you need.

> I'm grateful for every hint :-)

IMHO, this should help ;-)

> Ralf

Regards,
Thomas Weyergraf

