John,
     memory channel uses dedicated cabling between systems, or dedicated
cabling to a central hub (or three+ systems); it is documented as running
up to 100MB/sec (thats megabytes, not bits) with latency about 1/100th
that of 100Base-T networks.  Its quite expensive, so unless it happened
o come in a server you picked up, you probably won't see it.

>>  Most Alphas can share a common
>>  SCSI bus as a shared storage interconnect (network still required),

> OK.  Will this work with any old SCSI controller ?  These have NCR 810
> chips.

I do not have docs at all handy for this; you might check to see if the
OpenVMS website docs (www.openvms.compaq.com, look for documentation)
cover the details.  I do not think that the embedded controllers can be
used (because among other things they have the CD drive and all that in-
ternal cable length; the shared bus is disk only, no CD or tape allowed).
But the basic KZPAA (NCRyada810) _could_ be used as long as cable lengths
were kept short.  One sample setup I was once was a pair of Alphastation
200 systems with a BA353 pizzabox three-disk case shared.  I don't recall
if that was a supported config, but it did work.

VMS will not work with 'just any' controller.  You really need to use
DEC made or DEC badged (and firmware tweaked) units, _especially_ for
a shared bus setup.  No Adaptec, etc, and raid controller (even DEC
ones) cannot be used for shared bus.

> Even if you use windows, you don't have to use exchange and outlook !

But a distressing number of people and companies just default to it,
without much consideration or thought.  And get the results they should
have expected.

>>> IDE support ?

Until recently, IDE drives were made with office/home/light use in mind.
Even PC servers used SCSI; put even a decent IDE drive on a server and
it not only went slower, the drive would not live long under the con-
stant usage.  Cheap SCSIs like Fireballs had the same problem.

Now that IDE speed is getting to reasonable ranges, some manufacturers
are finally building drives that can live reliably in a server.  A 
recent phenomenon, and _so far_ saving a few hundred bucks on a
multi-thousand dollar server by going with a _probably_ somewhat slower
storage facility (under the type of workload our systems generate,
where SCSI capabilities continue to make a real difference, though
the gap has closed) is not worth it.

> Hmmm...  I've generally tended to use quite up market IBM drives, but
> who knows exactly what their SCSI capabilities are ?

IBM drives are mostly very good, though they also have (had) lower line
SCSI drives.  VMS issues were mainly VMS pickiness; when the IBM drives
reported a SCSI support revision or other data that VMS did not under-
stand, it offlined the drive.  The newer the VMS version, the more likely
it is to work with j-random SCSI device.

As far as graphics, AlphaServer systems generally had the base graphics
cards, like your number-9 units.  It purpose in life was to be the
console device of a multi-user application server, not a workstation
display.  If you ever want to you can probably upgrade to a Matrox
card (NT or Linux), or _possibly_ to a DEC TGA (pci) card, like a
ZLXp-E# (# = 1,2,or3) or ZLXp-L# (#=1,2); assuming those cards work
in a 2100 (I don't know).

Best of luck with your systems, whichever way you end up going with them.

Rich Jordan
rjordan@mcs.net



