As mentioned, Compaq is still declining to pursue small system/small
business sales except in certian niche markets.  There is support, but
it is diffuse.  The Q is happy to sell you a cluster of GS320s to run
your worldwide enterprise, but if you want a single DS10 to run your
single office small business all you will hear is reps whispering 
"Proliant" in your ear, even if VMS would be a better fit.  Major 
problem is marketing and perception within Compaq, which is still
overly wintel-centric and doesn't realize its supposed to have joined
the big time now.


>> unless you want to
>>play high end graphics

> :)  Games I can live without.

Not just games... but graphics aren't my forte either.  OTOH, the 
graphic hardware supported under VMS lately can be quite leisurely
when scrolling text windows at high resolution, which is annoying.
Some of the older TGA cards (ZLXp-E# and ZLXp-L#) were noticeably
faster than the Permedia cards (Elsa GLoria Synergy) used in newer
systems.  MAtrox need not apply, regretably.

>> or cheap pc-grade third-party SCSI devices

> So you have to use expensive drives ?  You can't just slam in a big
> generic SCSI disk lump ?

You take your chances.  Fireball-class drives are a crapshoot, but in
general it is because VMS uses a large part of the SCSI command set,
and _requires_ that devices it talks to follow the rules very tightly.
Its a reliability thing.  A device that cheats, misreports its
capabilities, doesn't change settings when told to, etc (all apparently
common problems with PC class drives, since a PC O/S would never
actually ask for those reports or generate those commands so the
drive mfgrs. take shortcuts) will all be unusable or unreliable under
the pounding VMS would give it.  Seagates tend to be OK in general.
Older IBM drives too.  Quantum Atlas drives have worked OK, but the
lower grade ones are data corruption waiting to happen, even though 
they work well under windoze or even Linux.

> IDE support ?

Not officially supported except for CDrom on some more recent units.
I'm not sure if the current systems support IDE hard drives for VMS
or not; we would never put a system out that way.

>>Compaq is doing better by VMS than Digital did under GQ Bob Palmer,
>>whose life ambition seemed to be to kill VMS and ruin Digital (so 
>>speaketh a stockholder!) but still niche-marketing it to very high-
>>end high reliability high availability (read expensive) sites and
>>limiting support at the middle and low end (though the low end boxes
>>are still great VMS systems).

>OK.  A little OS diversity won't harm the world.

The next time a single virus knocks out a significant number of systems
and email exchanges around the world, hopefully more people will come
to that same conclusion.

>If you get two systems, get the hobbyist licenses and media, and build
>a cluster you have a truly amazing environment with a fully distributed 
>lock manager and facilities that other OS's still can't match.
>
>Worth exploring.

> Maybe as a matter of interest, but probably a pretty narrow niche as a
> career.

Depending on area, there is a fairly significant demand for VMS talent
these days.  Folks haven't been graduating from college with VMS 
experience, but VMS is common in many large/critical environments, and
is in fact growing sales now; demand often exceeds the supply of people.

> Does clustering need any special hardware ?

For practically any recent system, all you need in hardware is a network
(ethernet) link between the systems.  Most Alphas can share a common
SCSI bus as a shared storage interconnect (network still required), or
use "memory channel" between nodes as a high speed interconnect.  But
good old 10Mbps ethernet will work fine.

