> So is 100 MHz FDDI any better than 100baseT  ?
>
>
John,
     FDDI in general is much more expensive to implement than ethernet.
It has better redundancy capabilities in a dual-ring arrangement, and
holds up better under peak loading conditions (because it is at heart
a token-based network).  Unless you get a deal on used networking
hardware and cables for FDDI, it may not be worth it.  

     Often older VAXen and the oldest Alphas only have 10Base-whatever
ethernet available anyway.  FDDI may be the only way to get 100Mbps
performance on the network side, if you really need it, and if the
VAX/Alpha in question has an adapter available (VAX 3100s do not; 
not sure if Qbus FDDI was ever made) but where you have a choice
between FDDI and 100Base the latter is a better buy in nearly all
cases.  

Rich Jordan
