The SuSE installation on my Alphastation500 and Alphastation200 worked fine 
for me, too.
I installed from CD, which is easier than using the floppy, as you're 
saying here.  All I had to do was (from SRM):
boot dka4 -fl 0
no need to change any of the system settings (ie bootdef_dev, bootfile, 
etc)

I wouldn't use alphabios if I were you, SRM console works just fine.  
Alphabios (as I understand it) is meant usually for booting NT.  If you're 
using unix, linux, or vms, srm is cool.

If you have to use the floppy instead of the CD, I suggest this boot 
command (from SRM):
boot dva -fl 0
I can say that, because you have said you've got /etc/aboot.conf on your 
floppy disk.
again, no need to change any of the system settings (ie bootdef_dev, 
bootfile, etc)

If you want to update your firmware (I suppose it can't hurt) you can get 
it from http://www.compaq.com.
Click "Download software and drivers"
In the section for Alphas, click on "Firmware for All Products"
Choose the system and product you are seeking.
Read the instructions.


