John Beardmore (wookie@wookie.demon.co.uk) wrote:
: In article <90e7ah$df8$1@pulp.srv.ualberta.ca>, Michal Jaegermann

: >BTW - RH 7 creates a default /etc/fstab with mounts by label.  This
: >still has a possibility of a screwup in other situations

: Such as ?

Labels created by default are "/" for /, "/usr" for /usr, and so on.
Anaconda tries to be smart about such things and if "/usr" label already
exists somewhere on the system it is supposed to write a little bit
different one (I had no opportunity to test that aspect of an installer)
so this is covered.  Still, if you take a disk from a system installed
that way and drop it into another similar one (not an everyday scenario
but not far-fetched for some types of an administrative work either)
then all of sudden you have two different partitions labelled "/usr" and
you try to mount both of those.  Oops!  Not the end of the world but it
may result in some confusion. :-)

UUIDs are supposed to be unique, or at least a probability of a clash
is a "practical zero".  Therefore in situations when disks move quite
a bit it could be better to mount things by UUID.

You can always relabel your disk partitions with 'tune2fs', and adjust
your /etc/fstab accordingly, but this may be not that much fun in
situations when you have tons of disks to work with.  OTOH rewriting
fstabs for UUID mounts can be trivially automated in a simple script.


: >: I tried tweaking /etc/fstab but it didn't help a whole lot -
: >: there were still other problems.
: >
: >???  Shrug!

: Well, that's what people here suggested because it worked for them.

"Shrug" was because "tweaking" may mean anything, including rewriting it
to mount by UUID or label,  and my crystal ball was just returned for
some warranty repairs and who knows when it will be back.  Maybe never.

  Michal



