Marble Head (nobody@nowhere.com) wrote:

: The advantages aren't as easy to list, but here are some...

[ various good reasons snipped ]

Here is few more:

  - If you hose a filesystem on some partition (a power bump, for
    example) then your system as a whole is not gone and much easier to
    recover.  As proper / is rather static chances that is gone are small
    and a system should be able to boot mounting only /.
  - It is easier to backup and restore things by partition and you
    can be more selective in that if you have many partitions. Some
    backup tools, like 'dump', work only on whole partitions.
  - If you have /tmp mounted from a separate partition then you can
    mount it 'noexec,nosuid' (nice security benefits) and overflowing
    /tmp will not hose your system because / is at 110% usage.
  - If you put /var and /tmp on their own partitions then it is easy
    to arrange things to mount /, and possibly /usr and /usr/share,
    read-only or even from read-only media like CD.  Nice for servers
    with a high exposure to the wide Internet.
  - If you are using NFS then things are exported by a partition
  - It is quite possible that some partitions (/home?) are mounted,
    or even automounted, from one common server
  - This list can grow...

In general you better control what is going on on your system.

   Michal



