I had a DEC AlphaStation 400 with the 233 MHz CPU. Putting in a Matrox
Millenium video card made it tolerable to use for most things. Not fast,
but it was fun to play with.

It was also built very well. Quite a bit better than the average
workstation.

You might look and see if you can get one of these. They have the Avanti
chipset IIRC, and came in several CPU speeds. Takes parity RAM, and put
in as much as you can; that helped a lot too. SCSI built in, ethernet, a
PCMCIA adapter included, and it originally had a huge 24-plane video
adapter that XFree didn't support. Video bus is PCI, so a number of
standard PC video cards will work like Matrox and S3.

I'd be willing to bet that these are out there on the used market. I
can't imagine that you could wear one out, short of running it over with
a truck. :-)

You should probably avoid the Multia. I've been told that they're just
not worth buying anymore. Too slow and not very expandable.

