How to partition your system is up to you.  Each choice you make has 
advantages and disadvantages.  

In each case, the biggest disadvantage to using a separate partition is:
- Will I run out of space on this partition?

The advantages aren't as easy to list, but here are some...
- If you have users, or anonymous FTP space, you might want to make /home 
separate, to avoid system choking from user space overload.
- If you want a multi-os system, you might want to make /boot separate, and 
share the same /boot among all os'es.  (maybe)
- You'll have to choose your swap space based on expected memory 
requirements and available physical ram.
- /usr usually grows the biggest, so you might want to use the whole second 
disk for that.
- If you expect heavy file i/o in some particular location, you should put 
your swap space on the other disk.  Faster total throughput that way.  
Also, you might want to use two separate swap partitions.  One on each 
disk.

....
For simplicity, here's what I did on my (also ancient Alphastation 200 with 
2 disks of 1gig) system: 
First Disk:  80 megs swap, nearly a gig /
Second Disk: 80 megs swap, nearly a gig /usr
That way, I've got a large amount (proportionally) of scratch space 
everywhere except /usr.  Easy to manage.



