No, it's NOT an Alpha system.  It's a VAX.

It _might_ run Linux; see <http://linux-vax.sourceforge.net/> for more
details; I would think that to be Not The Most Effective Use for it.  

NetBSD is likely a tad more usable than Linux.  And VMS is likely a
whole lot more usable than either NetBSD or Linux, and coincidentally
happens to be installed on the machine already.

You might want to look to some of the VMS literature on the web:
  <http://eisner.decus.org/vms/faq.htm>
  <http://dmoz.org/Computers/Software/Operating_Systems/VMS/>
  <http://home.earthlink.net/~djesys/vms/hobbyist/multia.html>
  <http://www.montagar.com/hobbyist/>
  <http://www.decus.org/>

OpenVMS is generally indicative of running VMS on Alpha hardware.

Note that there is POSIX environment stuff that runs on VMS, and you
can run a lot of the GNU tools like GCC, Emacs, and such there.  It's
decidedly different from Unix; you might well find that educational...
