Why bother?

Because uemacs is a very nice editor, and people are used to it. 

I use uemacs myself, and I consider it the best editor around.  Not for
any real technical reasons, but simply because I'm used to it.  Oh, and
it's not a buggered piece of overgrown sh*t like GNU emacs.  There. 
I've said it. 

I've put the version of uemacs I use on

	ftp.kernel.org:/pub/linux/kernel/uemacs

and the main thing about this version is that I got rid of all the old
termios/termio support, and write a "posix" line discipline module. The
makefile is set up correctly for this, and it should work fine on alpha.

		Linus

NOTE NOTE NOTE! Statement of modesty and legal issues: uemacs was
written and is copyrighted by Daniel Lawrence.  The particular version I
use (and put out for ftp) has been modified mainly by Petri Kutvonen at
University of Helsinki, based on version 3.9e.  My part in all of this
is mainly just the POSIX terminal handling, and extending some buffers
to be able to gracefully handle large windows etc. 

Neither I not Petri Kutvonen claim copyrights for the modifications, so
Daniel Lawrence is the copyright holder, and he allows for
non-commercial distribution only of uemacs (although if you ask him
nicely he would probably allow you to put it on a distribution CD or
similar use - but I cannot speak for him).



